sentences.l:
and first Menoetius’ sturdy son
then, as Areilycus turned, struck him in the thigh
with his sharp spear, and clean through drove the bronze;

And Phyleus’-son, having watched for Amphiclus as he rushed at him,
got in first, reaching at the leg at its root, where thickest
the muscle of a man is;

And Maris, at close quarters, with his spear
rushed at Antilochus, angered for his brother,
having taken his stand in front of the corpse;

but him godlike Thrasymedes
got in first, reaching, before he could wound, and did not miss,
the shoulder, at once;

Then Lycon on the
horse-plumed helmet’s ridge struck, and about the stem
the sword was shattered;
